and joining us now with more on the  recovery efforts in the Lone Star State  is Texas lieutenant governor Dan Patrick  lieutenant governor thank you so much  for being with us tonight for the very  important story so please tell our  viewers where you think that things  stand right now tonight in terms of the  rescue and the recovery efforts well the  storm is shifted as you know it started  in Rockport and near Corpus and it's  moved across the state about 300 miles  so now that's why you have the most  current and most important rescue going  on in Beaumont as you just report that's  where the water is the heaviest but the  rescue continues in Houston it was a  wind damage storm in the West and has  become a flooding storm we have about  35,000 people in shelters we think about  one out of every ten people leave their  home or in shelters that means three or  four hundred thousand people have been  displaced there with friends or family  or somewhere else some got hotel space  and then we have 14,000 Guard troops  deployed so we have security and rescue  efforts going on and we have about 500  roads that are still closed but I want  to thank President Trump who has been on  this before the storm hit we've gotten  just tremendous cooperation and service  from FEMA and the president governor  Abbott is doing a great job in command  and I'll tell you what Kimberly we are  Texas tough and as the word from  literature once said the worst of times  and the best of times this is the worst  of times for many but the best of times  by seeing the quality of the American  volunteer coming from other states and  the quality of the volunteerism of  Texans you saw that first responder  swimming next to that horse his life a  danger to save a horse all these  volunteers we've had our own Dunkirk  flotilla come in to save thousands and  thousands of people will never get the  official count I've bet ten to fifteen  thousand people have been rescued many  by volunteers and our brave first  responders what else do you need tonight  in your state in terms of viewers  watching at home what is the best that  way for them to direct their efforts to  provide assistance or help for those in  need  first of all give to either redcross.org  or Samaritan's Purse the charities that  we know are here on the ground and there  are many others but those are two we  need a lot of money tymberlee many of  these people didn't have flood insurance  they've lost everything the only thing  they have is the backpack on their back  their kids don't have any clothes for  schools the kids have lost their toys  the parents have lost their cars they've  lost their furniture but they have their  lives what is remarkable Kimberly the  biggest disaster probably in the history  of America natural disaster and every  life is precious but right now there we  think somewhere around three dozen  people have lost their lives that's a  miracle that is a miracle but we need  prayers we need the financial help but  remember this Kimberly right now in a  time when much of America was divided  they're all focused and and helping  Texas and in Texas there are no  Republicans tonight and no Democrat no  black white and brown or moderates or  conservatives we are one Texas helping  each other  Texas tough we'll survive will prevail  and we will lead to show others how to  take on these types of the death  disasters as we learn from this as we've  learned from disasters in the past you  know certainly Texas really truly a  shining example to the rest of the  country of what we can do when we come  together like you said in President  Trump when he was there echoing those  same you know comments and sentiments  that you're sharing with our viewers  tonight in terms of coming together  there are those that that criticize and  questions the president's action saying  that he didn't show empathy enough or  that he should have gone down to hug  victims of the ravaged or Mary but that  also presents somewhat of a logistical  problem when you have someone coming in  like a president and shutting down areas  where relief is still so sorely needed  well I saw the president on Tuesday and  had a chance to visit with him with the  governor and with our two senators  Cornyn and Cruz and I can tell you this  we all think he played it pitch perfect  he didn't get in the way of first  responders he didn't take away from the  effort he came in to Corpus where things  had settled a little bit they still have  issues there but he inspired the people  he came here to say I'm leading on this  issue what I like about the president  he's a business guy and this is he looks  at this and says we have a problem  what do we need to get back in business  what do we need to get the X is up and  running and and fix it and and he has  been all in from the declaration that  came so quick as he said about 35  seconds before the storm really hit he  and governor Abbott were close together  and I'm proud of our president I'm proud  of what they're doing but make no  mistake as brock long from fema who's  doing a great job for us here we know  this is going to be a long recovery  we still have problems as a report  showed it will be weeks before many  people get in their home months before  others and a year or more before  everything is back to the new normal but  I'm grateful for everyone our local  officials our county judges our mayor's  you can't imagine Kimberly every day we  have a briefing at 8 a.m. you can't  imagine the long list that we have to go  down just like Beaumont now you know  we're gonna have to supply water for  every citizen that's bottled water that  has to be trucked in I was in Victoria  yesterday I think it was and Kent the  days are all one and they is of  yesterday we were shipping in bottles of  water for 80,000 people that's a massive  effort to get in under time it is it's a  tremendous operation we've been through  it before nothing like this but again it  was the worst of times in the best of  times and I'm so proud to be a Texan and  I'm so proud of all Americans outside of  Texas who decided they want to be a  Texan you know I always tell people when  I give a speech no one moves to Texas  Kimberly they arrive here and Texas  moves into them I want to say Santa and  they want to stay and they and they do  by the way absolutely and just imagine  that just you know when I think about it  the luxury water what we take for  granted you know every day just so you  can have clean drinking water for those  that are sick the elderly and firmb  anybody to just be able to have that to  be able to survive and also water  continuing to rise in some areas so this  is far from over but as you say Texas is  strong and I understand the president is  coming back to see you I understand he's  coming in Saturday and I believe he's  coming in to Houston and by the way our  prayers go out to our friends in  Louisiana and other states that are  experiencing a heavy rain now moving up  through Tennessee and in other areas you  know Kimberly it's a it is a difficult  time for so many and and people have to  go to their new normal of you know if  they move to if they were displaced  where their kids go to school how they  get their benefits if their senior  citizens their Social Security check all  of those things you know you have to get  back running at life but we'll do that  you know you can buy new stuff and you  can repair sheetrock and get new carpet  but you can't replace your life and  people I think are so grateful just to  be to be alive  and to be alive I was out with one of  those rescue trucks in a neighborhood  with high water the other day and no one  knew I was on the truck I'm just helping  the volunteers who were volunteers they  had this old army truck and a lady came  up to be in a neighborhood Kimberly that  was pretty desolate it was like a ghost  town  and we're looking for people who wanted  to get out and she came up and she  happened to recognize me  it was my old senate district where i  used to be a senator and she said if I'd  known you were coming I'd have made you  dinner mr. lieutenant governor very  sweet and I thought that's what a great  what a great spirit she had and I said  don't you want to get on the truck she  said no I rescue cats I've got 45 cats I  don't my cats to drown I'm okay I'll be  okay and she said I'm going to do the  backstroke back to my house and that's  the kind of spirit everywhere you see  any informational stories you know it's  it's really true all right Gerry thank  you and Fox for everything you all have  been doing you've been putting focus on  this and and we really appreciate it we  really appreciate it certainly in our  job to do that and bring it to the  viewers at home all right lieutenant  governor thank you so much for sharing  these stories you bring us some insight  tonight

